# Pronunciation paired with the wrong name (birth/legal/alternate name)

## ==Original Issue==

Discovery titles (from the Vital-Articles list) often don't match the phrase
whose pronunciation the article's *first* `{{IPAc-en}}`/`{{respell}}` template
transcribes. The extractor grabs the first template it finds in the lead, but
that first template is frequently attached to a *different name in the lead*
(birth name, legal name, alternate spelling, a synonym), not the article title.

### Concrete examples (from resources/output-v4-p500-oct24-938am.tsv)

- `Stevie Wonder`  -> `STEEV-lənd`  (birth name "Stevland")
- `Stan Lee`       -> `ˈliːbər`     (birth surname "Lieber")
- `Madonna`        -> `Ciccone`     (birth surname Ciccone)
- `Uluru`          -> `Ayers`       (colonial name Ayers Rock)
- `Puncak Jaya`    -> `Carstensz`   (former colonial name)
- `Checkers`       -> `draughts`    (British synonym)
- `Desertion`      -> `AWOL`        (abbreviation treated as the pronunciation target)

These look *plausible* but pair the title with a pronunciation for a different
word/name, so the dictionary entry is wrong.

## Investigation notes (agent, kept for context)

Checked live leads: the "Stevie Wonder" lead's first template transcribes
"Stevland" (legal name `Stevland Hardaway Morris`, born `Stevland Hardaway
Judkins`); the stage name "Stevie Wonder" has no template in the lead at all.
"Stan Lee" similarly puts `{{IPAc-en|ˈliːbər}}` next to the birth name
"Lieber". So the *scope* information (which name an editor's template
describes) is not a machine-readable datum on the page; it's signaled only by
proximity in prose and by human knowledge.

## ==Status Report: 2026-10-24 18:00 US/Pacific==

Investigated whether the target name can be recovered deterministically. It
cannot in general: the lead is free prose, the bolded `'''...'''` name may itself
be the birth/legal name rather than the stage/common title, and the title's own
pronunciation may be absent from the lead title. A robust fix would require
understanding which word each template attaches to (proximity + dialogue about
name types), i.e. an NLP/LLM pass — not a mechanical rule. Left for human
review; likely a candidate for the post-extraction LLM cleanup idea.
